speechtext:
In answer to my hon. friend I would say that the Canadian Government ships have not been opening up trade routes. To every port where the Canadian Government Merchant Marine ships have gone since they were launched, other Canadian vessels have been going for years past. These routes have been served by other lines of Canadian steamships for 150 years past. It is all nonsense to say that these ships of the Canadian Government have increased Canadian business; they are running all around looking for freight. In the Halifax Herald of to-day my hon. friend will find it stated that one of the Government ships is leaving Halifax to go to Portland. Another one is bringing a cargo from Buenos Aires to Philadelphia. That is the way in which these steamers of the Canadian Government Merchant Marine are helping Canadian trade. The
[Mr. Duff.}
minister tells us what great things they are doing in the way of developing Canadian trade. I would like my hon. friend to turn to the report of the Department of Trade and Comemrce and see what evidence he can find there of that. The minister has a line of ships running to Brazil, and to hear him talk a minute ago one would imagine that nobody ever took any business to Brazil from Canada before. Why Canadian sailing ships have been going from St. John, Halifax, and-other Canadian ports to Brazil for the last one hundred and fifty years. My hon. friend from St. John knows that. It is not the Government owned ships only that have been doing this business.

The term "decayed " pilot is an old nautical term that we got from Trinity House very many years ago. The Quebec pilots have no more reason, on general principles, to receive a pension than the pilots in any other district of Canada, but when Mr. Justice Brodeur was Minister of Marine and Fisheries, an Order in Council was passed to the effect that when any of the Quebec pilots reached the age of sixty-five they should be placed on the retired list and given a pension of $300 a year. I do not see how the Govern-
[Mr. Ballantyne.}
ment can relieve themselves of the response-bility devolving upon them under the agreement that was entered into at that time betwe'en the then Minister of Marine and the Quebec pilots. My own opinion is that the agreement ought to be continued.
